      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange the four team members (Jordan, and his three friends, Alex, Ben, and Cindy) while following the given order: Jordan runs the fourth lap, while the others run in the first, second, and third laps respectively.\n\nSince Jordan has to run the fourth lap, we are left with 3 positions to fill for Alex, Ben, and Cindy. The number of different ways to arrange 3 items is given by the factorial of 3, often denoted as \\(3!\\).\n\nThe value of \\(3!\\) is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\nThus, there are 6 different ways to arrange Alex, Ben, and Cindy while keeping Jordan in the fourth position.\n\nTherefore, the number of different orders in which the team members can run, assuming Jordan runs the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
